                                Uni-Ed v6.2

                Written by Ray Akey for C-Net Amiga v2.18g+

WHAT IS IT?
-----------
 Uni-Ed is the ultimate User Editor, Access Group editor and **NOW** user
lister, in one program.  It is capable of doing many things in one program
instead of using 3 or 4 different C-Net commands (of which, some C-Net
doesn't have).


Why?
----
 Simply because it makes better sense to have these two functions side by side
where you can reference between the two without having to run two seperate
programs and because all info is editable at the touch of a few keys instead
of making you hit [RETURN] 15 times just to change that one flag that you wish
to change.


Installation
------------
1. Copy Uni-Ed to your pfiles
2. Logon and x[A]dd or Adopt (AO) Uni-Ed to your C-Net pfiles in C-Net as a
   [C]Net pfile.
3. Copy Uni-Edtext to your SYSTEXT: directory.
--

Uni-Ed's text is editable by loading Systext:Uni-Edtext into your favorite
text editor and making the changes to that file until it suits your tastes.

You can replace EA or EG with it as all changes to access groups are saved and
put into effect immediately after the changes are made.  I used "UE" and to
add it as UE you do the following:

1. Search for the LAST line in the "Maintenance Commands" section of BBSMENU.
2. add the following line after your last "Maintenance Command":

  UE, UEd                        |\#2Pfiles:Uni-Ed\

That's it, just reload C-Net and the command is now active.


Functions:
----------
 The only EA functions that aren't supported in this version (but WILL
be eventually) are editing DATES.  As I said this WILL be 
added in a future release.  All other functions are supported fully!

 Edit User
 ---------
           If you make no changes, nothing will be touched.  After making a 
 change to any account, the program senses this and saves the new info via
 CNet's own routines.  Give it a look.  If you don't trust it, backup your
 sysdata:bbs.udata file..  I guarantee it will not harm it in any way though.

 Edit Access Group
 -----------------
         Lists access group numbers and names immediately upon entry to
 avoid guessing for those who are like myself and can never remember what
 those "barely" used access groups are.
         Has every existing EG option covered!  After changing
 anything in the Access group you are editing/viewing, it will be saved and
 put into effect immediately... Also, you will be asked whether or not you wish
 to transpose the new info to ALL users (will be selectable in next version)
 with that same access number/group.  

  Again, if no changes are made, nothing is changed in your access groups.
 Changes are only made if YOU change them.

 TRANSPOSE NOTE:  Custom accounts will be overwritten so if you do not wish a 
 custom account to be changed, then bypass my transpose and use the one that
 has been provided with  C-Net .. I  just added this as a possible means of
 making things easier and a bit quicker for you.  I don't have any custom
 accounts on my system so I never really worked on this part yet.. I know
 there ARE some sysops who do though so, I will be making very extensive
 changes/additions in the future, In *ALL* areas of the program.

 There is no fancy cursor movement (no offense) to make the screen update
 seemedly faster since it is meant as a sysop program and, since the sysop
 *usually* logs in on a local port, I decided to bypass the Aerobic cursor :)
 thus making the program code shorter and more efficient.


USER LIST:
----------
  Uni-Ed now features a somewhat configurable User Lister.

  Entering "UL" at the main Uni-Ed prompt will bring you to a prompt where
you will be able to choose "F" to edit several flags regarding what the
UserList outputs.  If no flags are set, you will AUTOMATICALLY be taken to
the Flag setting screen.  Whatever flags you set will be saved as defaults
for the next Uni-Ed UserList session.

  Once you are done setting the flags, hit return to save as defaults and
then you will be taken back to the OUTPUT prompt.  Hitting return here
starts the userlist.

  To abort the listing, once started, hit the "\" key (not the "/" !!)

  Much more work will be going into this function and I will take suggestions
regarding the flag configuration and what you wish the UserList to support
as output.  Please see the section at the bottom of this doc if you wish to
contact me regarding ANY Uni-Ed function, bug, or suggestion.

